<nav class="screen-nav" aria-label="Workbook"><a href="/try/">First-session guide</a> · <a href="/workbooks/spectrum-basic-first-session/learner.pdf">Download this edition as PDF</a> · <a href="/try/workbook/teacher/">Teacher notes (contains answers)</a></nav><article class="workbook-root"><section class="sheet"><h1>Make the Spectrum answer</h1><p class="edition">Spectrum BASIC classroom workbook · Learner edition · Version 0.1 · 17 October 2026</p><p class="lead">Make a small program display words you chose. Predict the result, change one instruction and explain what happened.</p><div class="result"><p>One greeting you could make</p><pre>Hello, explorers!
to you
from the Spectrum</pre><p>The first message is your choice. This is text output, not a screen capture.</p></div><h2>Get ready together</h2><p>Use a browser and internet connection. A computer with a keyboard gives you room to read and edit. You do not need an original Spectrum or a local installation.</p><p>Open <a href="https://code198x.com/try/spectrum-basic/">code198x.com/try/spectrum-basic/</a> and follow <strong>Open the lesson</strong> to <strong>Run an editable listing</strong>. Use that listing for this workbook. You do not have to type the program into the Spectrum keyboard.</p><p>Working in pairs? One person edits and runs; the other predicts and checks. Swap after the first change. You can explain by speaking, writing or drawing.</p><p>If the tools will not run, trace the program on paper and mark your answers as predictions. Test them on the machine when you can.</p><footer class="page-foot"><span>Code Like It’s 198x · BASIC first session · v0.1</span><span>1 / 4</span></footer></section><section class="sheet"><h2>Read, predict and change</h2><p>The starting program has three stored lines. <code>PRINT</code> displays the text between quotation marks. Each instruction here finishes with a new line.</p><pre><code>  10 PRINT &quot;Welcome&quot;
  15 PRINT &quot;to you&quot;
  20 PRINT &quot;from the Spectrum&quot;</code></pre><div class="answer"><p>Before running: which messages do you expect, and in what order?</p><div class="writing-line" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="writing-line" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="writing-line" aria-hidden="true"></div></div><p>Choose <strong>Run this code</strong>. Compare the result, then choose <strong>Back to the listing</strong>.</p><h3>Make one deliberate change</h3><p>Replace only <code>Welcome</code> in line 10 with your own short greeting. Keep the quotation marks, line number and other two instructions.</p><div class="answer"><p>Write your changed line 10. Which message should change? Which should stay the same?</p><div class="writing-line" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="writing-line" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="writing-line" aria-hidden="true"></div></div><p>Run the edited listing. Swap roles and explain the result to each other.</p><div class="answer"><p>What appeared? Which instruction explains it?</p><div class="writing-line" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="writing-line" aria-hidden="true"></div></div><footer class="page-foot"><span>Code Like It’s 198x · BASIC first session · v0.1</span><span>2 / 4</span></footer></section><section class="sheet"><h2>Try the order experiment</h2><p>This is an optional extension. You can stop after your first working change.</p><p>In the <strong>editable text listing</strong>, change line number <code>15</code> to <code>25</code>. Keep its message. Leave lines 10 and 20 alone.</p><p>This edits the complete listing. Typing a new numbered line directly into a running Spectrum would add that line and leave line 15 stored until you delete it. Use the text listing here.</p><div class="answer"><p>Predict the three messages in their new running order.</p><div class="writing-line" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="writing-line" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="writing-line" aria-hidden="true"></div></div><p>Choose <strong>Run this code</strong>. Compare before looking for help.</p><div class="answer"><p>What happened? What decided the order in this program?</p><div class="writing-line" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="writing-line" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="writing-line" aria-hidden="true"></div></div><h3>If the screen disagrees</h3><ul><li>Read the error or unexpected output. Point to the first place it differs from your prediction.</li><li>Check quotation marks, line numbers and whether you ran the edited listing.</li><li>Use <strong>When the screen disagrees</strong> in the linked lesson. Ask for a hint whenever it helps.</li></ul><p>Before the next page, decide whether to keep your reordered program or restore line 25 to 15 in the text listing.</p><footer class="page-foot"><span>Code Like It’s 198x · BASIC first session · v0.1</span><span>3 / 4</span></footer></section><section class="sheet"><h2>Keep it, then explain it</h2><p>Copy your complete three-line listing into a plain text file, such as <code>greeting.txt</code>. Save it somewhere you can find again.</p><p>Reload the lesson. Open your saved file, copy its full listing and replace the whole editable listing in the lesson. Choose <strong>Run this code</strong> and check for your chosen words. This tests your kept source, not a fresh example.</p><p>You can also open <strong>Listing options</strong> and choose <strong>Download tape</strong> to keep a <code>.tap</code> program for an emulator with tape-loading controls. In this browser activity, reopen readable source by pasting it into the listing.</p><div class="answer"><p>Where did you save it? What did you check when reopening?</p><div class="writing-line" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="writing-line" aria-hidden="true"></div></div><p>Working on paper? Keep your changed listing and record that the machine result is still untested.</p><h3>Explain what you found</h3><div class="answer"><p>What does PRINT ask the machine to do?</p><div class="writing-line" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="writing-line" aria-hidden="true"></div></div><div class="answer"><p>How can one instruction change while the other messages stay the same?</p><div class="writing-line" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="writing-line" aria-hidden="true"></div></div><div class="answer"><p>What would you like to try or understand next?</p><div class="writing-line" aria-hidden="true"></div><div class="writing-line" aria-hidden="true"></div></div><p>You can stop when your change runs, you can explain it and you have kept a copy. There is no score or fixed time limit.</p><p class="credits">Adapted from Code Like It's 198x, <em>Make the Spectrum answer</em> and the first-session pack. Original and adapted prose: <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/">CC BY 4.0</a>; code samples: MIT, copyright (c) 2025 Code Like It's 198x. Sources and notices are on the final page of the teacher guide and in the accompanying source record.</p><footer class="page-foot"><span>Code Like It’s 198x · BASIC first session · v0.1</span><span>4 / 4</span></footer></section></article>
